{#-
  Shared skeleton for every capability workflow (spec 04 §2).

  Six capabilities differ only in their triggers and their scan steps; the
  header, the fail-fast probe, the concurrency group and the upload step are
  identical. Factoring them here means a change to the upload contract is one
  edit rather than ten, and it is why the templates are readable at all.

  Jinja variables are << like this >>, not {{ like this }}, because GitHub
  Actions expressions are ${{ ... }} and Jinja would otherwise evaluate every
  one of them. Block tags keep their default {% %} syntax, which does not
  collide with anything in Actions.

  The one cost of that choice: **no shell heredocs in these templates.**
  `cat <<JSON` opens with the same `<<` that starts a Jinja expression, and
  the template fails to compile with a syntax error pointing at the line
  after. Build strings with `printf` or `jq -n` instead — both are on every
  GitHub runner, and neither has the YAML indentation problems a heredoc
  inside a block scalar brings anyway.

  Children override: triggers, job_permissions, scan_steps, and optionally
  results_path / tool_name / job_name.
-#}
# Generated by Mykronos Workflow Installer — template version: << template_version >>
# Capability: << capability >> · Repo: << repo_full_name >>
#
# Do not edit by hand. A template resync will overwrite this file (spec 03 §6).
# To change behaviour, edit this repo's << capability >> capability config in
# Mykronos.

name: Mykronos << capability >>

on:
{% block triggers %}
  pull_request:
  push:
    branches: ["<< default_branch >>"]
  workflow_dispatch:
{% endblock %}

permissions:
  contents: read
{% block job_permissions %}{% endblock %}

concurrency:
  group: mykronos-<< capability >>-${{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: true

jobs:
  << capability >>:
    name: {% block job_name %}<< capability >>{% endblock %}

    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: << config.timeout_minutes | default(30) >>
{% block job_strategy %}{% endblock %}

    steps:
      - name: Check out the code
        uses: actions/checkout@11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262  # v4.4.0
        with:
{% block checkout_options %}
          # Full history: the adapter reads surrounding source to build the
          # snippet fingerprint that gives a finding stable identity
          # (spec 05 §5).
          fetch-depth: 0
{% endblock %}

      - name: Fail fast if Mykronos is unreachable
        # spec 05 §4. Better to stop in ten seconds than scan for twenty
        # minutes and find at upload time that the results have nowhere to go
        # — and silently skipping is not an option (spec 01 §6).
        #
        # Retried, because one slow answer is not an unreachable platform.
        # On 2026-08-30 seven lanes failed together on `curl: (28) Operation
        # timed out after 20002 milliseconds` while the ingestion host was
        # briefly saturated — it was serving in 0.3s a few minutes either
        # side. A single-shot probe turns a busy minute into seven red checks
        # and a scan that never ran, which is a worse answer than waiting.
        #
        # `--retry-all-errors` is what makes this work: `--retry` alone does
        # not retry a timeout, which is the exact failure observed.
        #
        # Still fails fast in the case it exists for. Three attempts at 15s
        # with a 5s backoff is under a minute, against the twenty a scan
        # would otherwise waste before discovering the same thing.
        run: |
          curl --fail --silent --show-error --max-time 15 \
            --retry 2 --retry-delay 5 --retry-all-errors \
            -H "Authorization: Bearer ${{ secrets.<< token_secret_name >> }}" \
            "<< ingestion_api_url >>/api/ingest/health" > /dev/null

{% block scan_steps %}{% endblock %}

      - name: Upload results to Mykronos
        # Shared composite action, semver-pinned: every onboarded repo depends
        # on it, so it must never be referenced by branch (spec 04 §2).
        # `always()` because a scan that failed still has to register its
        # ScanRun — "never ran" and "ran and broke" must stay distinguishable
        # in the lake (spec 04 §6, §7).
        if: always()
        uses: << upload_action_ref >>
        with:
          capability: << capability >>
          tool: {% block tool_name %}<< config.enabled_tool | default(default_tool) >>{% endblock %}

          results-path: {% block results_path %}${{ runner.temp }}/mykronos-results{% endblock %}

          ingestion-url: << ingestion_api_url >>
          token: ${{ secrets.<< token_secret_name >> }}
          severity-threshold: << config.severity_threshold | default('low') >>
          blocking: "<< config.blocking | default(false) | lower >>"
          # Same ref as the `uses:` line above, resolved when this file was
          # rendered. The action can also fall back to `github.action_ref`, but
          # that is empty inside a composite action, so it is not relied on.
          mykronos-ref: << mykronos_ref >>

{# A capability with an aggregate record as well as findings hangs it here:
   Atlas writes per-dependency Finding rows through the upload action above and
   its SscsEvidence summary after (spec 07 §3). Deliberately after, so a
   summary can never be recorded for findings that failed to upload. #}
{% block post_upload %}{% endblock %}
